WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.093 --> 00:00:04.578
Most teams run their LLM stack across

2
00:00:04.636 --> 00:00:05.913
three or four tools.

3
00:00:06.168 --> 00:00:09.047
The prompt lives in one, the traces in another,

4
00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:10.684
the bill somewhere else.

5
00:00:11.311 --> 00:00:14.469
Acrux Core is one platform for all of it,

6
00:00:14.655 --> 00:00:16.408
sharing one data model.

7
00:00:16.872 --> 00:00:20.205
Prompts are versioned data rather than strings in your

8
00:00:20.275 --> 00:00:20.797
code.

9
00:00:21.331 --> 00:00:24.791
One gateway endpoint sits in front of every provider,

10
00:00:25.011 --> 00:00:26.637
using your own keys.

11
00:00:27.032 --> 00:00:30.561
Because every call physically routes through that gateway, it

12
00:00:30.619 --> 00:00:33.498
is traced without you instrumenting anything.

13
00:00:33.963 --> 00:00:36.888
Tools get the same versioning prompts do, plus their

14
00:00:36.958 --> 00:00:38.153
own analytics.

15
00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:42.240
And evaluation datasets are built from real user feedback,

16
00:00:42.461 --> 00:00:44.899
not from fixtures somebody invented.

17
00:00:45.433 --> 00:00:47.000
Here is a real prompt.

18
00:00:47.221 --> 00:00:50.541
Version two is live in production, version one is

19
00:00:50.576 --> 00:00:53.885
still on staging, and moving that pointer needs no

20
00:00:53.966 --> 00:00:54.639
deploy.

21
00:00:54.976 --> 00:00:56.915
Every commit is diffable.

22
00:00:57.309 --> 00:00:59.330
Calling it takes one request.

23
00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:01.837
You send the prompt name and the alias.

24
00:01:02.174 --> 00:01:06.005
No messages, no model, no templating on your side.

25
00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:09.476
The reply carries the cost, the provider and the

26
00:01:09.523 --> 00:01:11.949
trace ID already attached.

27
00:01:12.576 --> 00:01:15.409
Accounting is not a batch job you run later.

28
00:01:15.746 --> 00:01:18.915
It rolls up here — spend, tokens, cache hits

29
00:01:18.939 --> 00:01:21.156
and errors for the whole team, with a badge

30
00:01:21.191 --> 00:01:23.757
on every call that came from a stored prompt.

31
00:01:24.291 --> 00:01:26.915
Open one and you get the model, the tokens,

32
00:01:26.973 --> 00:01:29.875
the cost, the captured payload, and a link to

33
00:01:29.933 --> 00:01:32.081
the exact prompt version behind it.

34
00:01:32.615 --> 00:01:34.484
Tools are first class as well.

35
00:01:34.775 --> 00:01:37.538
The catalog versions them, and this page gives you

36
00:01:37.631 --> 00:01:41.288
call volume, error rate and latency for each one.

37
00:01:41.682 --> 00:01:43.099
So how does that compare?

38
00:01:43.319 --> 00:01:46.384
We ran the same job through LangSmith, Langfuse

39
00:01:46.431 --> 00:01:49.891
and PromptLayer by hand, and three differences were

40
00:01:49.949 --> 00:01:50.761
structural.

41
00:01:51.295 --> 00:01:53.501
All three ask you to wrap your own model

42
00:01:53.559 --> 00:01:56.148
client so their SDK can watch it.

43
00:01:56.612 --> 00:01:59.027
Here the gateway is already in the path, so

44
00:01:59.109 --> 00:02:00.989
tracing is not a thing you add.

45
00:02:01.616 --> 00:02:03.520
None of them treat a tool as a governed

46
00:02:03.613 --> 00:02:04.356
object.

47
00:02:04.647 --> 00:02:08.594
A tool is per-call detail, never something you version

48
00:02:08.687 --> 00:02:09.767
and reuse.

49
00:02:10.301 --> 00:02:12.460
And none of them turn a thumbs down into

50
00:02:12.542 --> 00:02:16.222
drafted rewrites, judged against what is live, ready

51
00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:18.149
to promote in one click.

52
00:02:18.683 --> 00:02:20.622
One trade off, said plainly.

53
00:02:21.249 --> 00:02:24.709
Datasets here come only from real feedback, so a

54
00:02:24.755 --> 00:02:27.902
brand new account has nothing to evaluate until real

55
00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:29.191
traffic arrives.

56
00:02:29.818 --> 00:02:32.476
One platform, from the first prompt to the trace

57
00:02:32.523 --> 00:02:33.579
it produced.
